    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124574,
      "id": "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855",
      "title": "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "9f252204-297d-5d2f-aa28-6e3b0332c0a1",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)&=\\left(6 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(12 y z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(6 y^{2} + 4 z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=6 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=12 y z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=6 y^{2} + 4 z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(6 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=3 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=12 y z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=12 y z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(12 y z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=6 y^{2} z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=6 y^{2} z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=6 y^{2} z + 3 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=6 y^{2}+h'(z).\n$$\n\nComparing this derivative with the third component and subtracting the known terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(z)&=4 z \\\\ h(z)&=2 z^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=6 y^{2} z + 2 z^{2} + 3 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}+C.\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "9594152f-00e9-5faa-a4de-7d9d930653b8",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
