    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4cca82fc-7d38-51d8-9d9c-cb3c6f8cc9ee",
      ":question/problem": "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)&=\\left(- 6 x - 3 y e^{z}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(- 3 x e^{z} - 6\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(- 3 x y e^{z} - 6 z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=- 6 x - 3 y e^{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- 3 x e^{z} - 6 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=- 3 x y e^{z} - 6 z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(- 6 x - 3 y e^{z}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=- 3 x^{2} - 3 x y e^{z}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- 3 x e^{z}+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- 3 x e^{z} - 6$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 3 x e^{z}+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=- 3 x e^{z} - 6 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(-6\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=- 6 y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=- 6 y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- 3 x^{2} - 3 x y e^{z} - 6 y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=- 3 x y e^{z}+h'(z).\n$$\n\nComparing this derivative with the third component and subtracting the known terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(z)&=- 6 z \\\\ h(z)&=- 3 z^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- 3 x^{2} - 3 x y e^{z} - 6 y - 3 z^{2}+C.\n$$",
