    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5fa18897-207a-583f-ae2b-2d1e00b2e6f0",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)&=\\left(- 3 y e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(- 3 x e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(12 \\cos{\\left(2 z \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=- 3 y e^{x y} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- 3 x e^{x y} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=12 \\cos{\\left(2 z \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(- 3 y e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=- 3 e^{x y}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- 3 x e^{x y}+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- 3 x e^{x y}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 3 x e^{x y}+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=- 3 x e^{x y} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(0\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- 3 e^{x y}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=h'(z).\n$$\n\nComparing this derivative with the third component and subtracting the known terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(z)&=12 \\cos{\\left(2 z \\right)} \\\\ h(z)&=6 \\sin{\\left(2 z \\right)}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- 3 e^{x y} + 6 \\sin{\\left(2 z \\right)}+C.\n$$",
